== 2. Prime powers: a logarithmic tower with a signed response

The exact prime-power current is the von Mangoldt population

$
  g_("fin")''(t)
  = sum_(m>=2) frac(Lambda(m), sqrt(m))
    (delta(t-log(m))+delta(t+log(m))).
$

The machine-facing version is simpler to inspect: a prime power remains a
gear with ancestry `(p,k,p^k)`, not a generic composite.  The spectral world
retained examples such as $2^8=256$ with symbolic coordinate `[8,2]` and
$17^2=289$ with `[2,17]`.  The later derivation showed that the repeated
returns are the inverse Euler channel

$ J_p^(-1)=sum_(k>=0) p^(-k/2) U_(k log(p)) $,

and that the prime response is a derivative of the positive return metric.
That derivative has a sign; the positive metric does not automatically sign
the response.  This is why the prime-power tower is a promising RH carrier
and not already a positivity proof.

